    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0010",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 10,
    "stem": "Which type of antimicrobials require pre-authorization from an Infectious Disease Consultant or Antimicrobial Stewardship Clinician as a type of interventional strategy to control antimicrobial resistance?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Regular antibiotic"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Monitored antimicrobial"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Narrow spectrum antibiotics"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Restricted antimicrobial"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Restricted antimicrobials are those with limitations on their use due to factors such as potential resistance development or the need to preserve their efficacy. These antimicrobials typically require pre-authorization from an Infectious Disease Consultant or Antimicrobial Stewardship Clinician as an interventional strategy to control antimicrobial resistance. This process helps ensure that these drugs are used judiciously and in situations where their benefits outweigh the risks.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Regular antibiotics, also known as broad-spectrum antibiotics, are commonly prescribed without the need for pre-authorization. However, their use may be subject to review and guidelines to ensure appropriate prescribing practices.",
      "B": "Monitored Antimicrobials are drugs that are monitored but do not require pre-authorization from an Infectious Disease Consultant.\nExamples of monitored antibiotics include aminoglycosides, piperacillin/tazobactam, ticarcillin/clavulanic acid, fluconazole, third generation cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones, and clindamycin.",
      "C": "Narrow-spectrum antibiotics are often preferred in antimicrobial stewardship efforts due to their targeted activity against specific bacteria. While they are generally considered more selective, it does not typically need pre-authorization.",
      "D": "Restricted antimicrobials are those with limitations on their use due to factors such as potential resistance development or the need to preserve their efficacy. These antimicrobials typically require pre-authorization from an Infectious Disease Consultant or Antimicrobial Stewardship Clinician as an interventional strategy to control antimicrobial resistance. This process helps ensure that these drugs are used judiciously and in situations where their benefits outweigh the risks."
    },
