    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0032",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 32,
    "stem": "Prevention of hookworm is achieved by the following except .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Promoting the use of slippers and not to walk barefoot"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Perform mass deworming of the community"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Treat patients metronidazole and iron supplementation"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Promoting to use latrines and decreasing outdoor defecation"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "While metronidazole is an effective treatment for certain parasitic infections, it is not the first-line treatment for hookworm. Iron supplementation may be provided to individuals with hookworm-induced iron deficiency anemia. However, it is more crucial to focus on deworming medications, such as albendazole or mebendazole, for treating hookworm infections.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Hookworm larvae can penetrate the skin, often through bare feet, leading to infection. Promoting the use of slippers and avoiding walking barefoot is an effective preventive measure to reduce the risk of hookworm infection.",
      "B": "Mass deworming campaigns involve administering anthelmintic medications to an entire community to treat and prevent parasitic infections, including hookworm. This is a proactive approach to reduce the overall burden of infection.",
      "C": "While metronidazole is an effective treatment for certain parasitic infections, it is not the first-line treatment for hookworm. Iron supplementation may be provided to individuals with hookworm-induced iron deficiency anemia. However, it is more crucial to focus on deworming medications, such as albendazole or mebendazole, for treating hookworm infections.",
      "D": "Hookworm eggs are passed in human feces, and the larvae can contaminate the soil. Encouraging the use of latrines and reducing outdoor defecation helps minimize the environmental contamination with hookworm larvae, thereby preventing infection."
    },
