    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0034",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 34,
    "stem": "Scabies prevention include the following, EXCEPT .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Bedding, clothing, and towels used in the last three days should be washed in hot water and dried in a hot dryer"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Spraying fogs is advised in the community"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Treatment should include entire household"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Avoid skin contact with infected person"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "This is not a standard recommendation for scabies prevention. Scabies is primarily transmitted through direct skin-to-skin contact, and the use of fogs or sprays in the community is not considered an effective preventive measure. Focus is generally placed on personal hygiene and targeted treatment.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This is a recommended measure for scabies prevention. Washing items that have come into contact with an infested person, such as bedding, clothing, and towels, in hot water and drying them in a hot dryer can help kill scabies mites and their eggs, reducing the risk of transmission.",
      "B": "This is not a standard recommendation for scabies prevention. Scabies is primarily transmitted through direct skin-to-skin contact, and the use of fogs or sprays in the community is not considered an effective preventive measure. Focus is generally placed on personal hygiene and targeted treatment.",
      "C": "This is a crucial aspect of scabies prevention. Since scabies is highly contagious, it is essential to treat all household members, even if they are not exhibiting symptoms. Failure to treat everyone in close contact with an infested person can lead to reinfestation.",
      "D": "This is another important preventive measure. Scabies spreads through prolonged skin-to-skin contact with an infested person. Avoiding direct contact helps minimize the risk of transmission. It's particularly important during the treatment period to prevent the spread of mites."
    },
