    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0037",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 37,
    "stem": "In an outbreak investigation, case-control studies can provide the .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Odds ratio"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Sensitivity"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Relative risk"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Specificity"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Case-control studies are particularly well-suited for calculating odds ratios. The odds ratio (OR) measures the odds of having the disease in the exposed group (cases) compared to the odds in the unexposed group (controls).",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Case-control studies are particularly well-suited for calculating odds ratios. The odds ratio (OR) measures the odds of having the disease in the exposed group (cases) compared to the odds in the unexposed group (controls).",
      "B": "Sensitivity is not typically calculated in case-control studies. Sensitivity is more relevant in diagnostic testing scenarios, where it assesses the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals with the disease.",
      "C": "Relative risk (RR) is more commonly associated with cohort studies, not case-control studies. The formula for relative risk is used to compare the incidence of the disease in the exposed group to the incidence in the unexposed group. It is not a measure routinely calculated in case-control studies.",
      "D": "Specificity is not a measure commonly calculated in case-control studies. Specificity is more relevant to the evaluation of diagnostic tests, where it assesses the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals without the disease."
    },
