    "explanation": "While excessive alcohol consumption is associated with certain cancers, including lung cancer, an anti-alcohol campaign might not be a specific component of a lung cancer prevention program. Lung cancer prevention programs typically focus on tobacco control and smoking cessation, given that smoking is a primary risk factor for lung cancer.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Rules on labeling and advertising of cigarettes are typically part of comprehensive tobacco control measures. These rules aim to reduce the attractiveness of tobacco products and increase awareness of the health risks associated with smoking. In the context of a lung cancer prevention program, such rules are a relevant and effective component.",
      "B": "Prohibiting smoking in Department of Health (DOH) and Local Government Unit (LGU) premises is a direct and practical measure to create smoke-free environments. This aligns with lung cancer prevention efforts by reducing exposure to secondhand smoke and promoting a healthier environment.",
      "C": "While excessive alcohol consumption is associated with certain cancers, including lung cancer, an anti-alcohol campaign might not be a specific component of a lung cancer prevention program. Lung cancer prevention programs typically focus on tobacco control and smoking cessation, given that smoking is a primary risk factor for lung cancer.",
      "D": "An anti-smoking campaign is a core and essential component of a lung cancer prevention program. Smoking is a major risk factor for lung cancer, and campaigns aimed at reducing smoking prevalence, promoting smoking cessation, and raising awareness of the associated health risks contribute significantly to preventing lung cancer."
    },
