    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0046",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 46,
    "stem": "Select the endocrinopathic disease NOT detected by newborn screening test:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Phenylketonuria"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "G6PD deficiency"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Hypothyroidism"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Cystic fibrosis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Cystic fibrosis is not an endocrinopathic disorder; it is a genetic disorder affecting the respiratory, digestive, and reproductive systems. Newborn screening tests does not routinely include cystic fibrosis unless there are specific considerations. It is not typically classified as an endocrinopathic disease.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Phenylketonuria is a metabolic disorder where the body is unable to process an amino acid called phenylalanine. Newborn screening tests commonly include PKU because early detection and management are crucial to prevent intellectual disabilities. Therefore, PKU is typically included in newborn screening panels.",
      "B": "G6PD deficiency is not an endocrinopathic disease; it is an enzyme deficiency that affects the red blood cells. Newborn screening tests may not routinely include G6PD deficiency unless there are specific regional or ethnic considerations. It is not typically classified as an endocrinopathic disorder.",
      "C": "Hypothyroidism is an endocrinopathic disorder involving an underactive thyroid gland. Newborn screening commonly includes tests for congenital hypothyroidism, as early detection and intervention are crucial for preventing developmental issues. Therefore, hypothyroidism is usually part of newborn screening programs.",
      "D": "Cystic fibrosis is not an endocrinopathic disorder; it is a genetic disorder affecting the respiratory, digestive, and reproductive systems. Newborn screening tests does not routinely include cystic fibrosis unless there are specific considerations. It is not typically classified as an endocrinopathic disease."
    },
