    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0055",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 55,
    "stem": "What is the most critical step in waste reduction recycling?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Waste segregation"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Incineration"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Collection of solid waste"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Transfer and transport"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "This is correct. Waste segregation involves separating different types of waste at the source to facilitate recycling and proper disposal. It helps in maximizing the recovery of recyclable materials and minimizes the amount of waste that goes to landfills.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This is correct. Waste segregation involves separating different types of waste at the source to facilitate recycling and proper disposal. It helps in maximizing the recovery of recyclable materials and minimizes the amount of waste that goes to landfills.",
      "B": "Incineration is a waste treatment process that involves the combustion of organic substances contained in waste materials. While it can reduce the volume of waste and generate energy, it may not be considered the most critical step for waste reduction recycling, as it doesn't address the core principle of recycling and reusing materials.",
      "C": "Collection is an essential step in the waste management process, but it precedes waste segregation. The effectiveness of waste management depends on proper collection methods, but without segregation, it becomes challenging to manage waste sustainably.",
      "D": "Transfer and transport involve moving waste from collection points to treatment or disposal facilities. While essential for the overall waste management process, it is not the most critical step in waste reduction and recycling."
    },
