    "originalNumber": 79,
    "stem": "Post-menopausal women between 50-60 years old without breast cancer were included in the study. The occurrence of breast cancer in this group was monitored in the study. The occurrence of breast cancer in this group was monitored in the next 10 years. Select the appropriate research design for the objective.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Experimental study"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Cohort study"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Case series study"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Cross sectional study"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "A cohort study follows a group of individuals over time to observe the development of outcomes. In this case, post-menopausal women between 50-60 years old without breast cancer are followed over the next 10 years to monitor the occurrence of breast cancer. This matches the description of a cohort study.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Experimental studies involve the manipulation of an independent variable to observe its effect on a dependent variable. In this scenario, there is no mention of manipulation or intervention; the study is observing the occurrence of breast cancer over time.",
      "B": "A cohort study follows a group of individuals over time to observe the development of outcomes. In this case, post-menopausal women between 50-60 years old without breast cancer are followed over the next 10 years to monitor the occurrence of breast cancer. This matches the description of a cohort study.",
      "C": "A case series involves the collection of detailed information on a group of individuals with a particular condition or characteristic. It does not involve following individuals over time to observe outcomes.",
      "D": "Cross-sectional studies collect data at a single point in time and do not involve following individuals over time. This study is interested in monitoring the occurrence of breast cancer over the next 10 years, making it longitudinal rather than cross-sectional."
    },
