    "stem": "The difference between the expected frequencies and the observed frequencies is one or more categories is .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "ANOVA"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Logistic regression"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Cronbach’s Alpha"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Chi square"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "This is the correct option. The difference between expected and observed frequencies in one or more categories is a characteristic of the chi-square test. The chi-square test is used to determine if there is a significant association between two categorical variables. The test involves comparing observed and expected frequencies in a contingency table.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "ANOVA is a statistical method used to analyze the differences among group means in a sample. It is used when there are more than two groups to compare. ANOVA is not directly related to the difference between expected and observed frequencies in categorical data.",
      "B": "Logistic regression is a statistical method used for modeling the probability of an event occurring, especially in binary outcomes. It is not directly related to the difference between expected and observed frequencies in categorical data.",
      "C": "Cronbach’s Alpha is a measure of internal consistency reliability in a set of items or questions. It is used in the context of psychometrics and survey research. It is not related to the difference between expected and observed frequencies in categorical data.",
      "D": "This is the correct option. The difference between expected and observed frequencies in one or more categories is a characteristic of the chi-square test. The chi-square test is used to determine if there is a significant association between two categorical variables. The test involves comparing observed and expected frequencies in a contingency table."
    },
