    "id": "notion-preventive-medicine-0095",
    "subject": "preventive-medicine",
    "originalNumber": 95,
    "stem": "Select the vulnerable population:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "I, II"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "I, II, III"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "II, III"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "I, III"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Categorical Vulnerability – defines vulnerable populations as those groups in society whose members share features that might make them vulnerable. For example, the U.S. code of federal regulation lists “children, prisoners, pregnant women, mentally disabled persons, economically or educationally disadvantaged persons” as vulnerable groups.\nContextual Vulnerability - In its 2001 report, Ethical and Policy Issues in Human Subjects Research, the National Bioethics Advisory Commission proposed an alternative to the categorical definition of vulnerability, highlighting the extent to which vulnerability in research subjects is sensitive to context. NBAC described 6 types of vulnerability that could apply to research participants in different circumstances:\nCognitive or communicative vulnerability: young children, language barrier\nInstitutional Vulnerability: Prisoners, military personnel\nDeferential Vulnerability: Children or military personnel\nMedical vulnerability: having a serious health condition for which there is no satisfactory standard treatment\nEconomic Vulnerability: being disadvantaged in the distribution of social goods and services such as income, housing or health care\nSocial vulnerability: being a member of an undervalued or disenfranchised social group",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This is incorrect.",
      "B": "Categorical Vulnerability – defines vulnerable populations as those groups in society whose members share features that might make them vulnerable. For example, the U.S. code of federal regulation lists “children, prisoners, pregnant women, mentally disabled persons, economically or educationally disadvantaged persons” as vulnerable groups.\nContextual Vulnerability - In its 2001 report, Ethical and Policy Issues in Human Subjects Research, the National Bioethics Advisory Commission proposed an alternative to the categorical definition of vulnerability, highlighting the extent to which vulnerability in research subjects is sensitive to context. NBAC described 6 types of vulnerability that could apply to research participants in different circumstances:\nCognitive or communicative vulnerability: young children, language barrier\nInstitutional Vulnerability: Prisoners, military personnel\nDeferential Vulnerability: Children or military personnel\nMedical vulnerability: having a serious health condition for which there is no satisfactory standard treatment\nEconomic Vulnerability: being disadvantaged in the distribution of social goods and services such as income, housing or health care\nSocial vulnerability: being a member of an undervalued or disenfranchised social group",
      "C": "This is incorrect.",
      "D": "This is incorrect."
    },
